            <ArticleTitle>[The relationship between the duration and severity of asthma].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>475-81</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The relationship between the duration and severity of asthma has not been comprehensively investigated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We examined the relationship between duration of asthma and asthma severity among 4776 patients with asthma: 608 with childhood-onset persistent asthma (12.7%), 187 with adolescent-onset asthma (3.9%), and 3981 with adult-onset asthma (83.4%). The assessment of asthma severity was based on the Japanese Guidelines for The Diagnosis and Treatment of Allergic Diseases, 2007 (JGL 2007).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Asthma severity was assessed at 2 years, 3 to 10 years. 11 to 20 years, and 21 years or longer after disease onset. Disease severity significantly increased with greater asthma duration (p&lt;0.001). This relationship was not affected by serum total IgE concentration (&lt;300 IU/ml vs &gt; or = 300 IU/ml), smoking status, house dust mite-specific serum IgE antibody level, or gender.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Our findings suggest that bronchial asthma is a disease that worsens over time.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
